In order to create and use React in the future, few steps must be followed.

1. Install all the vital libraries to allow your machine to have full access.
2. Install Tailwind as it makes styling easier and more intuitive.

Now moving to the pages themselves.

1. index.html comes first (heart)
    It is the heart of your project and all the other pages will be injected in this only page
    Try to see this guy as an empty screen which will display all the information required without asking you to move
    <head><body> and the root element are born here
    A link to the next step is also created here src/main.jsx

2. src/main.jsx comes second (squeleton)
    Here we start to import the crucial elements which will make our application safer and more efficient
    StrictMode, createRoot and index.css are the 3 main keys for this to be reliable
        One responsible for safety, another the engine which translates JS to JSX and the syling with Tailwind
    "createRoot(document.getElementById('root')).render" is created to track and "capture" the root instantiated on the first step
    Now <StrictMode> englobes everything to display any error message to us
        <App> also makes its first shown here. This is the "director" in charge to decide who will be shinning now.
            (what page will be displayed this time)

3. To conclude the holy trinity src/App.jsx comes (brain)
    Here will be decide what page will shine. The director comes into action
    import { BrowserRouter as Router, Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom' | PrivateRoute | NavBar
        The main one being the first, which decides what page will appear on screen.
        Every other global component must be imported here
    In addition, all other pages being used on the applpication MUST be imported here as well
    const App = () => a function without arguments is created and inside of it, the routes are stablished
        <Router> leads the way, englobing everything.
            <NavBar> follows as a way to save memory
            <Routes> encapsulates the route of every page imported earlier (<Route path="/" element={<HomePage />} />)
            p.s Routes accessible by anyoone are created as above
            => private routes only accessible by certain users must be more strict 
            (<Route path="/dashboard" element={<PrivateRoute><DashboardPage /></PrivateRoute>} />)
    After that, we export default App to be used

//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
useEffect => used ONLY when something must happen automatically, without the user touching or acting in someway
    usually useful to sync the site with external elements (database, APIs, etc...)
Ways to control it:
    [] Empty Array => the code will run only once when "spaws" on screen
    [variable] With a Variable inside the array => React keeps the track of that specific variable, once it changes, it runs the effect again to sync.
    p.s => NEVER leaves an useEffect without an array, otherwise It'll break the code and run infinitely
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
useState => A way to store data and draw what the user must be seing.
    if the changing of a data demands a visual update for the user, useState must be used
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
useRef => Used to save settings that must be remembered by the page but doesn't need to be displayed on screen
    if we want to keep the track of an element, but don't need to user to see it being changed, we use useRef
//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
const () => => The most common and popular way to create a function in JS
    safer to use than its predecessor (var) since It doesn't allow the function to be rewritten afterwards accidentaly
    if such acction is tried, JS throws an error and doesn't allow the code to go through
const is also used to store variables that will be rendered immediately after loading the screen.
const before () => {} is a Function Declaration, defining a rule or action that the website can execute at any time
//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
=> src/components
    Components are like reusable parts of code used to save space and time on your pages.
    Whenever you need something that will be essential in most pages, we create a new component.
    Navbar, LanguageSelector and LoadingSpinner are good examples.

Navbar as a practical example
    1. Respectives imports to be used into the file
    2. Component's signature | const NavBar = () => {} | Simple way to assign ad create simple functions (EVERY SINGLE FUNCTION WILL BE INSIDE THIS GUY)
        The imported const's will be reassigned here | const navigate = useNavigate() |

